# Mendel’s Four Principles of Heredity

@body

- [(1) Principle of Paired Factors]{color=red}
  - [Each organism has two alleles that make up the genotype for a given character]{color=red}
- [(2) Principle of Dominance]{color=gray}
  - [In each genotype there is a dominant allele, the phenotype is determined by dominant allele]{color=gray}
- [(3) Law of Segregation or Law of Purity of Gametes]{color=gray}
  - [In sexual reproduction, each parent contributes just one of their alleles to its offspring]{color=gray}
- [(4) Law of Independent Assortment]{color=gray}
  - [different pairs of alleles are passed onto the offspring independently of each other.]{color=gray}
  - [inheritance of genes at one location does not influence the inheritance of genes at another location.]{color=gray}

=== layout: one-panel

# Key Features of Single Gene Inheritance

@body

- Genes come in pairs so each cell has two alleles of a gene
- For each pair of genes, the alleles may be the same or different
- Each gamete (sperm or egg) contains only one allele of each gene

